1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,407
Hello! This is our last lesson.

2
00:00:02,407 --> 00:00:08,930
The friends who walked twenty-four weeks '함께' [ham-kke] (together) have gathered in one place.

3
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:15,671
This is a design hall in Seoul — not one straight line in it.

4
00:00:15,671 --> 00:00:18,447
We didn't come in a straight line either.

5
00:00:18,447 --> 00:00:21,020
We '배웠어요' [bae-wo-sseo-yo] (learned) by winding around.

6
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,996
Three parts today: we look back at the letters,

7
00:00:26,996 --> 00:00:28,435
then the street words,

8
00:00:28,435 --> 00:00:30,268
then the words of the heart.

9
00:00:30,268 --> 00:00:32,100
And then we '수료' [su-ryo] (graduate).

10
00:00:37,330 --> 00:00:42,320
First, the three drawn in lines: 졸라맨, 졸라걸, 스틱맨.

11
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:47,310
Letters are lines too, so they suit each other.

12
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:51,537
In week one we learned how Hangeul was born — heaven,

13
00:00:51,537 --> 00:00:52,393
earth and human.

14
00:00:52,393 --> 00:00:54,320
The letters came out of those three.

15
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:03,631
Consonants copy the shape of the mouth: 'ㄱ' [g] the root of the tongue,

16
00:01:03,631 --> 00:01:06,330
'ㅁ' [m] the lips. Trace them with me.

17
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:17,125
Put a consonant and a vowel together and you get one syllable block: '가' [ga],

18
00:01:17,125 --> 00:01:18,010
'나' [na], '다' [da].

19
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:25,366
One more piece underneath is a 받침 (final consonant).

20
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:30,050
That part '어려웠어요' [eo-ryeo-wo-sseo-yo] (was hard) — but you did it.

21
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:35,164
Now numbers: 하나, 둘, 셋.

22
00:01:35,164 --> 00:01:44,280
Korean counts two ways — 하나 둘 셋, and 일 이 삼.

23
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:51,555
To ask the time, say '몇 시예요? ' [myeot si-ye-yo] (what time is it?

24
00:01:51,555 --> 00:01:56,390
): 한 시, 두 시, 세 시. Repeat after me.

25
00:01:59,050 --> 00:02:03,250
Just then someone calls from outside — the other friends had already gone out.

26
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:10,998
인준 and 지은 learned the words you use outside — in shops,

27
00:02:10,998 --> 00:02:13,380
on the street, on the bus.

28
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:19,294
In a shop you ask '얼마예요? ' [eol-ma-ye-yo] (how much is it?

29
00:02:19,294 --> 00:02:22,503
) — the shortest way to ask a price.

30
00:02:22,503 --> 00:02:23,930
Repeat after me.

31
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,093
If you want it cheaper,

32
00:02:29,093 --> 00:02:33,840
say '깎아 주세요' [kka-kka ju-se-yo] (please give me a discount) — better with a smile.

33
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:39,115
On the street you ask '어떻게 가요?

34
00:02:39,115 --> 00:02:41,105
' (how do I get there?

35
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:43,820
): by subway, by bus, on foot.

36
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:53,606
To give directions: '왼쪽으로 가세요' [oen-jjo-geu-ro ga-se-yo] (go left),

37
00:02:53,606 --> 00:03:00,410
'오른쪽으로 가세요' [o-reun-jjo-geu-ro ga-se-yo] (go right), '곧장 가세요' [got-jjang ga-se-yo] (go straight).

38
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:03,663
Below ground here, a Joseon-era wall remains.

39
00:03:03,663 --> 00:03:06,840
The new stands on the old — study is like that too.

40
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:12,668
When you ask about hobbies,

41
00:03:12,668 --> 00:03:15,580
you also ask how often.

42
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:20,390
'자주' [ja-ju] (often) is an adverb: 자주, 가끔, 전혀.

43
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:23,691
At the end of the path, on the grass hill,

44
00:03:23,691 --> 00:03:25,300
two people were already sitting.

45
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:34,870
마담제이 and 티쳐제이 learned how to speak the heart — the slow kind of talk you have sitting down.

46
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:43,130
Describe your day: 일어나요 (get up), 먹어요 (eat),

47
00:03:43,130 --> 00:03:50,940
일해요 (work), 자요 (sleep). In order, it's easy.

48
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:55,471
For weather: 맑아요 (it's clear),

49
00:03:55,471 --> 00:04:01,320
비가 와요 (it's raining), 바람이 불어요 (it's windy).

50
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:04,040
Today is a fine one.

51
00:04:04,490 --> 00:04:09,934
You can speak feelings too: 기뻐요 (I'm glad),

52
00:04:09,934 --> 00:04:14,492
속상해요 (I'm upset), 설레요 (I'm excited).

53
00:04:14,492 --> 00:04:17,910
Saying it makes it lighter.

54
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:19,891
To give an opinion,

55
00:04:19,891 --> 00:04:25,130
start with '제 생각에는' [je saeng-ga-ge-neun] (in my opinion) — it sounds gentle and clear.

56
00:04:27,430 --> 00:04:32,012
To agree: '저도 그렇게 생각해요' [jeo-do geu-reo-ke saeng-ga-kae-yo] (I think so too) — short,

57
00:04:32,012 --> 00:04:34,630
but it brings people closer.

58
00:04:36,950 --> 00:04:39,239
We talked about travel too.

59
00:04:39,239 --> 00:04:43,394
For experience: '가 본 적이 있어요' (I have been there).

60
00:04:43,394 --> 00:04:45,090
I have been to Jeju.

61
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:59,100
And last week: '약속을 잡다' [yak-sso-geul jap-tta] (set up an appointment) and '시간 조율' [si-gan jo-yul] (coordinating times) — you need both to meet as a group.

62
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:06,190
Now all seven are together — sitting in one classroom for the first time in twenty-four weeks.

63
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:13,759
Now we '수료' [su-ryo] (complete the course) — it means you've finished the whole thing.

64
00:05:13,759 --> 00:05:15,010
Repeat after me.

65
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:22,578
When your name is called, come and receive it.

66
00:05:22,578 --> 00:05:26,507
The giver says '축하합니다' [chu-ka-ham-ni-da] (congratulations),

67
00:05:26,507 --> 00:05:29,670
the receiver '고맙습니다' [go-map-sseum-ni-da] (thank you).

68
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:35,127
Someone was watching from the front all along — the man who made Hangeul.

69
00:05:35,127 --> 00:05:37,870
'덕분에' [deok-ppu-ne] (thanks to) him, we came this far.

70
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:46,678
Now you can say it: '자신 있어요' [ja-sin i-sseo-yo] (I feel confident).

71
00:05:46,678 --> 00:05:51,780
And say it to each other too: '잘했어요' [jal-hae-sseo-yo] (well done).

72
00:05:52,230 --> 00:05:57,314
Now let's go outside — today is a celebration.

73
00:05:57,314 --> 00:06:00,630
It keeps going '앞으로' [a-peu-ro] (onward),

74
00:06:00,630 --> 00:06:02,840
'계속' [gye-sok] (continuously).

75
00:06:04,870 --> 00:06:06,232
You made it this far.

76
00:06:06,232 --> 00:06:09,021
Now it's your turn — go on with confidence.

77
00:06:09,021 --> 00:06:09,670
Thank you!

